From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CogWasm <cogwasm@example.invalid>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 16:30:00 +1030
Subject: [PATCH] wasm: give std a placeholder process id

rustc's self-profiler calls `std::process::id()` to make its output file
name unique. On wasm32-wasip1 that reaches `sys::process::unsupported` and
panics before the front end runs, so a warm edit with `-Zself-profile`
cannot be profiled inside the serving guest at all. The panic also happens
inside measureme's `Profiler::with_counter`, so avoiding the call in rustc
alone is not enough.

A serving guest runs one profiled session at a time, so return the constant 1
on wasm and keep the explicit panic on every other unsupported platform. The
profile file name keeps its existing shape, `{crate}-{pid}.rustc_profile`,
which measureme writes as `{crate}-{pid}.mm_profdata`.

Default compiler behaviour is unchanged: the wasm branch only runs when a
caller asks for a process id. This is a local CogWasm experiment, not an
upstream contribution.

---
diff --git a/library/std/src/sys/process/unsupported.rs b/library/std/src/sys/process/unsupported.rs
index f7d7f489e..255381bc4 100644
--- a/library/std/src/sys/process/unsupported.rs
+++ b/library/std/src/sys/process/unsupported.rs
@@ -332,5 +332,13 @@ pub fn read_output(
 }
 
 pub fn getpid() -> u32 {
-    panic!("no pids on this platform")
+    // wasm32-wasip1 has no process id. The serving rustc uses it only to name
+    // self-profile output, and runs one profiled session at a time, so a
+    // stable placeholder lets that code path work without changing anything
+    // else; every other platform keeps the explicit panic.
+    if cfg!(target_family = "wasm") {
+        1
+    } else {
+        panic!("no pids on this platform")
+    }
 }
